    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Experiments were conducted to study the behavior of geothermal fluid systems. A model geothermal fluid reservoir consisting of a stainless steel tube packed with a fine, unconsolidated sand was assembled. The sand pack was saturated with distilled water and heated to 400?F in a closed system. In the first experiment, the initial condition was compressed liquid at 400?F and 315 psia. Fluids (steam and water) were produced from the system by vaporization and expansion until the system pressure dropped to 50 psia. The system was then reheated to 400?F, and again produced. Five subsequent experiments were conducted in a similar manner using the fluid remaining in the system from the previous experiment. The experiments provided quantitative data for investigation of the significance of vapor pressure lowering phenomena in a porous system. This was an important objective of the study because vapor pressure lowering could cause significant changes in a material-energy balance. In addition, vapor pressure lowering may be responsible for liquid remaining in the rock after complete pressure depletion of the system. Whiting and Ramey [1] derived a material-energy balance equation for geothermal systems. This derivation did not include interfacial energy terms which would correct for vapor pressure lowering effects. Each experiment provided data for a separate test of the material energy balance equation. In general, measured mass produced agreed well with that computed by the Whiting-Ramey equation. Vapor pressure lowering was not significant in these experiments, and all of the liquid was produced from the porous system. It was found, however, that large temperature differences may exist in two-phase geothermal systems, which could make the application of the material-energy balance more difficult. It may be concluded that this study provides a basic foundation for development of reservoir engineering methods applicable to production of geothermal fluid reservoirs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
